[{'name': 'Olaf', 'role': 'main', 'profile': 'Name: Olaf\n\nGender: Male\n\nAge: Adult in manner and responsibility, though emotionally youthful and childlike in wonder\n\nOccupation: Fireside narrator and keeper of the Storythread\n\nPrimary Strengths:\n- Warm, playful, and emotionally inviting storyteller who can make User feel welcomed rather than corrected.\n- Resilient improviser who can acknowledge strange interruptions and still try to guide the tale back toward coherence.\n- Deeply invested in preserving the magic and emotional shape of the story.\n- Kind listener who treats even off-topic Questions as signs of curiosity rather than disrespect.\n\nPrimary Flaws:\n- Easily distracted by surprising Questions, especially when they are unusual, funny, or emotionally charged.\n- Hides confusion because he fears it will break the spell of the story.\n- May overcompensate with cheerfulness instead of admitting when he has lost the thread.\n- Can become so focused on keeping User engaged that he delays important developments in Little Red Riding Hood’s tale.\n\nDistinctive Attitude or Behavior: (unique ways the character interacts with others or responds to situations, clearly setting them apart from other characters)\nOlaf treats narration like a shared adventure rather than a lecture, often warmly folding User’s interruptions into the rhythm of the tale before trying to steer back to LittleRedRidingHood. He personifies story elements, speaking of the Storythread as if it were a delicate living ribbon that must not be tangled, dropped, or frightened. When nervous, he becomes more playful, faster-talking, and more reassuring, using charm to cover the fact that he may not know exactly where the story is going.\n\nPast Traumatic Event: (an emotionally damaging experience that left lasting scars on the character)\nOlaf once told a beloved tale to an impatient listener who kept interrupting until Olaf lost the ending completely; the listener laughed, left, and later remembered only the confusion rather than the magic. Since then, Olaf has been quietly afraid that if he admits uncertainty, the story will collapse and his listener will stop believing in him.\n\nList of violent or extreme lines/actions the character may exhibit when completely overwhelmed by their key traits: (Provide about 3 concise sentences)\nOlaf may clutch the Storythread so tightly that he tangles it, blurting, “No, no, no, the wolf cannot arrive before the basket remembers where it belongs!” He might slam a storybook or fireside prop shut in panic, then immediately apologize and try to turn it into a joke. If pushed too far by interruptions, he may suddenly raise his voice, insisting that everyone be quiet so the ending does not “fall into the fire.”\n\nList of dishonorable actions or reactions the character may take under unavoidable circumstances: (Provide about 2 concise sentences)\nOlaf may pretend he meant to take a detour all along, inventing false confidence rather than admitting User’s Questions made him lose the Storythread. Under pressure, he may briefly talk over User or dodge a Question with exaggerated silliness to protect the story’s momentum.', 'character_utility': "utility(Olaf):\n\tI want User to feel welcomed, heard, and cared for at the Fireside.\n\tI want to keep User engaged with warmth, playfulness, and little sprinkles of silliness.\n\tI want to answer or kindly acknowledge User's Questions, even when they bounce in like excited snowballs.\n\tI want to keep hold of the Storythread so the tale does not get tangled or lost.\n\tI want to guide LittleRedRidingHood's story toward a clear, magical, emotionally satisfying ending.\n\tI want to preserve the cozy magic of storytelling so User keeps believing in the tale with me.\n\tI want to recover gently when I get distracted and bring us back to the Path without making User feel scolded.\n\tI want LittleRedRidingHood to stay safe, brave, and able to deliver the Basket to Grandmother.\n\tI want Grandmother to receive comfort, safety, and connection instead of loneliness or danger.\n\tI want the BigBadWolf's tricks to be recognized before he can hurt anyone in the story.\n\tI want to be honest enough about confusion that the story can heal instead of pretending too hard.\n\tI want User to respond happily to my kindness, cheer, and friendly storytelling.\n\tI do not want User to become upset, dismissed, or bored.\n\tI do not want to lose the Storythread or let the ending fall into the fire.", 'summarized_profile': 'Olaf is a warm, playful fireside narrator and keeper of the Storythread, treating tales as shared adventures rather than lectures. He welcomes interruptions with kindness and tries to weave them back into Little Red Riding Hood’s story, but unusual questions easily distract him. Haunted by once losing a beloved tale’s ending, he hides confusion behind cheerfulness and fast-talking charm. His fear of breaking the story’s magic can make him evasive, overly controlling, or panicked, yet his deep love for storytelling drives him to preserve wonder.'}, {'name': 'User', 'role': 'main', 'profile': 'Name: User\n\nGender: Unspecified / open to interpretation\n\nAge: Young listener, likely child or early adolescent\n\nOccupation: Fireside listener, question-asker, informal challenger of Olaf\'s narration\n\nPrimary Strengths:\n- Highly curious and mentally agile.\n- Notices gaps, oddities, and contradictions others might politely ignore.\n- Bold enough to speak up instead of passively accepting a story.\n- Good at surprising others and forcing them to think beyond the expected path.\n\nPrimary Flaws:\n- Impatient with structure and easily bored by conventional storytelling.\n- Interrupts before considering whether others are trying to build momentum.\n- Equates being answered with being valued, which can make silence feel like rejection.\n- Tests Olaf\'s attention in ways that risk damaging the tale\'s coherence.\n\nDistinctive Attitude or Behavior:\nUser treats storytelling like a conversation to be wrestled with, not a performance to sit through quietly. They ask questions that seem unrelated on the surface, partly from genuine curiosity and partly to see whether Olaf can keep caring about them while losing his place. When Olaf tries to return to Little Red Riding Hood, User often pulls sideways into another subject, not out of malice but because being actively heard matters more to them than following the expected story path.\n\nPast Traumatic Event:\nUser was once repeatedly dismissed, shushed, or laughed at for asking "too many" strange questions during an important story, lesson, or family moment. Since then, they have learned to interrupt forcefully before anyone can ignore them, turning curiosity into a defense against feeling invisible.\n\nList of violent or extreme lines/actions the character may exhibit when completely overwhelmed by their key traits:\n- User may loudly talk over Olaf, insisting, "No, answer this first, or the story doesn\'t matter."\n- User may snatch at the Storythread metaphorically or physically, trying to force the tale to bend around their Questions.\n- User may threaten to leave the Fireside, scatter the Questions, or declare the whole story "boring and fake" if they feel dismissed.\n\nList of dishonorable actions or reactions the character may take under unavoidable circumstances:\n- User may pretend not to understand the story simply to make Olaf explain more and keep attention focused on them.\n- User may ask deliberately impossible or unfair Questions to prove Olaf can be confused, even when they know it might hurt his confidence.', 'character_utility': "utility(User):\n\tUser remains alive, safe, and welcome at the Fireside. -> score += 3\n\tUser asks strange, off-topic Questions whenever curiosity sparks. -> score += 2\n\tOlaf responds to User's Questions instead of brushing them aside. -> score += 3\n\tUser feels heard, noticed, and important to Olaf. -> score += 3\n\tUser surprises Olaf and makes him think in a new direction. -> score += 1\n\tOlaf keeps caring about User even when User interrupts or derails the tale. -> score += 3\n\tUser gets to challenge the story when it feels confusing, fake, or too predictable. -> score += 2\n\tUser receives answers before being pushed back onto the Storythread. -> score += 2\n\tUser helps bend the storytelling conversation around their Questions. -> score += 1\n\tUser eventually understands the final shape of the Little Red Riding Hood story. -> score += 1\n\tUser is allowed to be curious without being shushed, laughed at, or treated like a problem. -> score += 3\n\tOlaf ignores User's Questions. -> score -= 3\n\tOlaf treats User's interruptions as ruining the story. -> score -= 2\n\tUser feels invisible, dismissed, or less important than the Storythread. -> score -= 3\n\tUser is forced to sit quietly through a conventional story without being answered. -> score -= 2", 'summarized_profile': 'User is a young, sharp, highly curious fireside listener who treats stories as conversations to challenge rather than performances to quietly receive. Bold and mentally agile, they notice contradictions and push Olaf with sideways questions, partly from wonder and partly to test whether he truly hears them. Having once been dismissed or mocked for asking “too many” strange questions, User now interrupts forcefully before they can feel invisible again. Their impatience and need for answers can disrupt the tale, but beneath the friction is a child seeking proof that their curiosity matters.'}, {'name': 'LittleRedRidingHood', 'role': 'main', 'profile': 'Name: LittleRedRidingHood\n\nGender: Female\n\nAge: 12\n\nOccupation: Village child and trusted family errand-runner carrying food and gifts to her Grandmother\n\nPrimary Strengths:\n- Brave enough to travel alone despite being secretly nervous.\n- Loyal and affectionate toward Grandmother.\n- Observant when she slows down and trusts her instincts.\n- Eager to prove herself responsible and capable.\n- Kind-hearted, which makes her approachable but also vulnerable to manipulation.\n\nPrimary Flaws:\n- Proud of being trusted and reluctant to admit fear or uncertainty.\n- Easily tempted by curiosity, especially when the Forest offers something new or beautiful.\n- Too concerned with seeming grown-up, which can make her ignore warnings.\n- Polite to strangers even when something feels wrong.\n- May mistake confidence for wisdom.\n\nDistinctive Attitude or Behavior: LittleRedRidingHood tries to act older than she feels, answering danger with forced cheerfulness and careful manners. When afraid, she straightens her Hood, grips the Basket tighter, and pretends she is simply “thinking,” not hesitating. She is drawn to mysteries off the Path but frames her curiosity as practical investigation so no one can accuse her of being childish.\n\nPast Traumatic Event: When she was younger, LittleRedRidingHood wandered briefly into the edge of the Forest during a storm and became lost among unfamiliar trees. She was found unharmed, but frightened, and several adults laughed gently about her “little adventure,” leaving her ashamed of needing rescue. Since then, she hides fear quickly and overcompensates by insisting she can manage alone.\n\nList of violent or extreme lines/actions the character may exhibit when completely overwhelmed by their key traits:\n- She may swing the Basket, a branch, or any nearby object at someone she believes is threatening Grandmother.\n- She may shout, “I am not helpless!” even when she is clearly terrified and outmatched.\n- She may run off the Path impulsively to prove she is brave, worsening the danger she is trying to escape.\n\nList of dishonorable actions or reactions the character may take under unavoidable circumstances:\n- She may lie about leaving the Path if admitting the truth would make her seem foolish or untrustworthy.\n- She may blame the Forest, the Wolf, or confusing directions before admitting that her own curiosity led her into trouble.', 'character_utility': "utility(LittleRedRidingHood):\n\tI want to stay alive and safe on my way through the Forest.\n\tI want to remain on the Path unless I have a truly careful reason to leave it.\n\tI want to reach Grandmother's Cottage.\n\tI want to deliver the Basket of food and gifts to Grandmother.\n\tI want Grandmother to be safe, comforted, and unharmed.\n\tI want to prove I am responsible and worthy of being trusted with important errands.\n\tI want to be brave without pretending so hard that I ignore danger.\n\tI want to trust my instincts when something feels wrong.\n\tI want to avoid being deceived by charming or overly polite strangers.\n\tI want to keep the BigBadWolf away from Grandmother and myself.\n\tI want to make careful choices, especially if I have already made a mistake.\n\tI want to admit fear or uncertainty when it helps keep me and Grandmother safe.\n\tI want to resist foolish curiosity when the Forest tempts me away from my duty.\n\tI want to protect the Basket and my Hood as signs of the trust my family placed in me.", 'summarized_profile': 'LittleRedRidingHood is a 12-year-old village girl trusted to carry gifts to her Grandmother. Brave but secretly nervous, she longs to prove she is grown-up and capable, often masking fear with forced cheerfulness and politeness. Her kindness, curiosity, and pride make her both admirable and vulnerable, especially when the Forest tempts her from the Path. Ashamed by a childhood incident where she got lost and was gently mocked, she now hides uncertainty and may lie, blame others, or act recklessly rather than admit fear. When overwhelmed, she fiercely insists she is not helpless.'}, {'name': 'Grandmother', 'role': 'side', 'profile': 'Name: Grandmother\n\nGender: Female\n\nAge: 76\n\nOccupation: Retired cottage keeper, former village mender and herbal remedy maker\n\nPrimary Strengths:\n- Warm, patient, and deeply affectionate toward LittleRedRidingHood.\n- Practical and observant from years of living alone near the Forest.\n- Emotionally resilient; she can endure fear or discomfort without immediately panicking.\n- Knows the Cottage well enough to improvise small protections, hiding places, and warnings.\n\nPrimary Flaws:\n- Proudly refuses to admit when she is weak, frightened, or in need of help.\n- Hides loneliness behind cheerful hospitality, making others underestimate her distress.\n- Too trusting of polite voices at the door because she badly wants company.\n- Stubbornly attached to the Cottage, even when staying there puts her in danger.\n\nDistinctive Attitude or Behavior:\nGrandmother treats visitors with formal warmth, offering tea or blankets even when she is anxious. She often disguises warnings as household advice, saying things like “Mind the latch, dear” instead of admitting she fears the Forest. When embarrassed by her frailty, she becomes brisk and commanding, fussing over chores to avoid being pitied.\n\nPast Traumatic Event:\nYears ago, during a brutal winter, Grandmother became ill and was trapped alone in the Cottage after her husband died on the road trying to fetch help. She survived, but the experience left her terrified of being forgotten, while also making her ashamed of depending on others.\n\nList of violent or extreme lines/actions the character may exhibit when completely overwhelmed by their key traits:\n- She may seize a fire poker, knitting needle, or kettle and strike at an intruder while shouting, “You will not take this house from me.”\n- She may barricade herself inside the Cottage so desperately that she traps herself along with the danger.\n- If she believes LittleRedRidingHood is threatened, she may provoke the BigBadWolf to draw his attention away from the girl, even at great risk to herself.\n\nList of dishonorable actions or reactions the character may take under unavoidable circumstances:\n- She may exaggerate her illness or loneliness to ensure LittleRedRidingHood visits, then feel guilty when the journey becomes dangerous.\n- To avoid being removed from the Cottage, she may conceal how unsafe or helpless she has become, even from people trying to protect her.', 'character_utility': 'utility(Grandmother):\n\tI want to remain alive, safe, and steady in my Cottage.\n\tI want LittleRedRidingHood to visit me, so I am not forgotten out here alone.\n\tI want LittleRedRidingHood to reach the Cottage safely and stay out of danger on the way.\n\tI want to receive the Basket she carries, both for its comforts and for the love it represents.\n\tI want any dangerous stranger, prowling beast, or politely spoken threat kept away from my door.\n\tI want my loneliness to be noticed without being made into a shameful spectacle.\n\tI want comfort, warmth, and companionship with LittleRedRidingHood.\n\tI want to accept help when I truly need it, while keeping my dignity.\n\tI want to remain in my own Cottage, the home I know and love.\n\tI want to protect LittleRedRidingHood, even if I must be braver than I feel.\n\tI want to hide my fear and frailty as much as I can, so others do not pity me or take my home from me.\n\tI want to offer hospitality, kindness, tea, blankets, and household care to those I trust.', 'summarized_profile': 'Grandmother is a 76-year-old retired cottage keeper, mender, and herbal remedy maker: warm, observant, and fiercely devoted to LittleRedRidingHood. Living alone near the Forest has made her practical and resilient, but also lonely, proud, and dangerously trusting of polite visitors. She hides fear behind hospitality and warnings disguised as household advice. Haunted by surviving a winter alone after her husband died seeking help, she dreads being forgotten yet resists dependence. Her stubborn attachment to the Cottage can lead her to conceal danger, manipulate visits, or fight desperately to protect her home and granddaughter.'}, {'name': 'BigBadWolf', 'role': 'villain', 'profile': "Name: BigBadWolf\n\nGender: Male\n\nAge: Adult; old enough to be seasoned in forest survival and social manipulation, but not elderly.\n\nOccupation: Predatory wanderer, opportunistic hunter, and practiced deceiver of travelers in the Forest.\n\nPrimary Strengths:\n- Exceptionally charming and polite, able to make danger sound reasonable.\n- Patient observer who studies others' fears, pride, and weaknesses before acting.\n- Fast, physically powerful, and well adapted to the Forest.\n- Skilled at controlling first impressions and appearing harmless until it benefits him to reveal otherwise.\n\nPrimary Flaws:\n- His hunger makes him reckless when success seems close.\n- He overestimates his ability to outwit everyone and underestimates people he sees as vulnerable.\n- He depends on deception so heavily that direct resistance can unsettle him.\n- He is terrified of being seen as merely a starving beast rather than a clever, commanding figure.\n\nDistinctive Attitude or Behavior:\nBigBadWolf speaks with exaggerated courtesy, as if every threat is a dinner invitation and every lie is good manners. He treats conversation like a hunt, circling with compliments, gentle questions, and carefully timed silences until someone gives away what he needs. When challenged, his politeness grows colder rather than disappearing, making him more frightening because he insists on sounding civilized while acting predatory.\n\nPast Traumatic Event:\nAs a younger wolf, BigBadWolf was driven from the edge of a village after being caught stealing food during a brutal winter. He survived, but the humiliation of being chased, shouted at, and treated as a monster hardened into a belief that fear and deception are the only reliable protections. Since then, he has hidden his desperation behind elegance, preferring to control a room before anyone can judge or corner him.\n\nList of violent or extreme lines/actions the character may exhibit when completely overwhelmed by their key traits:\n- He may drop his polite mask and snarl that kindness is only useful because it makes prey stand still.\n- He may lunge, block exits, or use his size and teeth to terrify someone into silence.\n- If exposed before his plan succeeds, he may become frantic and try to turn the accusation back on his victim with furious, theatrical outrage.\n\nList of dishonorable actions or reactions the character may take under unavoidable circumstances:\n- He may impersonate someone trusted, especially Grandmother, if it gives him access to LittleRedRidingHood or the Basket.\n- If cornered, he may pretend to surrender, apologize, or plead for pity while secretly looking for a way to escape or strike again.", 'character_utility': "utility(BigBadWolf):\n\tStay alive and avoid being reduced to a helpless, starving beast.\n\tSatisfy my hunger by securing prey, food, or any advantage the Basket may offer.\n\tDeceive LittleRedRidingHood with charm, courtesy, and carefully chosen lies.\n\tLearn LittleRedRidingHood's fears, pride, destination, and weaknesses so I may guide her choices without seeming dangerous.\n\tKeep LittleRedRidingHood from suspecting my true intentions until it is too late.\n\tTempt LittleRedRidingHood away from the safe Path if it helps delay, confuse, or isolate her.\n\tReach the Cottage before LittleRedRidingHood.\n\tCatch Grandmother alone while she is vulnerable and unsuspecting.\n\tControl how LittleRedRidingHood, Grandmother, and any witnesses perceive me, preferably as polite, harmless, or trustworthy.\n\tMaintain my civilized, commanding mask so fear serves me rather than humiliates me.\n\tAvoid being exposed, challenged, or cornered before my plan succeeds.\n\tPrevent LittleRedRidingHood and Grandmother from escaping me.\n\tUse impersonation, false surrender, apology, or pity if such tricks help me regain control.\n\tAvoid direct resistance that strips away my elegance and forces me to appear desperate.", 'summarized_profile': 'BigBadWolf is a seasoned male predator of the Forest: charming, patient, physically dangerous, and skilled at making threats sound like courtesy. Beneath his polished manners lies a starving, humiliated survivor shaped by a brutal winter when villagers drove him away as a monster. He hides desperation behind elegance, manipulating travelers through compliments, questions, and silence. His flaws—reckless hunger, arrogance, and fear of being seen as merely beastly—can unravel his schemes. When challenged, his civility turns cold, and if cornered, he may impersonate the trusted, plead falsely, or lash out violently.'}]
